import{_ as a,o as s,c as n,ah as t}from"./chunks/framework.ojwJKwrc.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Runtime Semantics","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"internals/cascade-limitations.md","filePath":"internals/cascade-limitations.md"}'),o={name:"internals/cascade-limitations.md"};function i(c,e,l,d,r,p){return s(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[t(`<h1 id="runtime-semantics" tabindex="-1">Runtime Semantics <a class="header-anchor" href="#runtime-semantics" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Runtime Semantics&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Celox uses event-driven execution with split evaluate and commit phases. This page defines the runtime ordering model for simultaneous and cascaded clock events.</p><h2 id="simultaneous-domains" tabindex="-1">Simultaneous domains <a class="header-anchor" href="#simultaneous-domains" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Simultaneous domains&quot;">​</a></h2><p>All clock domains triggered at one simulation time observe the same committed state. Celox first evaluates their next-state logic into the Working region and only then commits all results to the Stable region.</p><div class="language-veryl vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">veryl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>assign gclk = clk;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>always_ff (clk) {</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    cnt1 = cnt1 + 1;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>always_ff (gclk) {</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    cnt2 = cnt2 + cnt1;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>}</span></span></code></pre></div><p>When <code>clk</code> and <code>gclk</code> trigger together, <code>cnt2</code> reads the pre-update value of <code>cnt1</code>. Evaluation order therefore does not change the result.</p><p>Only a copy of a whole variable joins its source&#39;s domain. A clock taken from part of a wider signal, such as <code>let divided: &#39;_ clock = counter[0];</code>, is a derived clock of its own and is edge-sensed on the selected bit.</p><h2 id="gated-clocks" tabindex="-1">Gated clocks <a class="header-anchor" href="#gated-clocks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Gated clocks&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A clock computed combinationally from another clock&#39;s signal changes at the same instant as that signal:</p><div class="language-veryl vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">veryl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>let gated: &#39;_ clock = clk &amp; en;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>always_ff (clk) {</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    cnt1 = cnt1 + 1;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    en = en_next;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>always_ff (gated) {</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    cnt2 = cnt1;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>}</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Celox follows the SystemVerilog scheduling of the code Veryl emits (IEEE 1800-2023 4.4.2, 4.5), for both frontends. When <code>clk</code> rises, <code>clk &amp; en</code> is evaluated in the same active region as <code>clk</code>&#39;s processes, before the nonblocking updates: its edge is judged from the state before the edge with <code>clk</code> at its new level, and its domain evaluates together with <code>clk</code>&#39;s as a simultaneous domain. <code>cnt2</code> reads the pre-update <code>cnt1</code>, and the edge is gated by the <code>en</code> from before it.</p><p>The nonblocking updates then re-evaluate the gate. If the edge raised <code>en</code> while <code>clk</code> is still high, <code>gated</code> rises after the commit; that is a further edge, handled in the cascade rounds below, and its domain reads the committed state. A gate that falls after the commit is a falling edge in the same way. When <code>clk</code> returns low, clocks such as <code>~clk</code> or a falling edge of <code>gated</code> are judged from the state the rising edge left, as a separate instant of the timed <code>Simulation</code>.</p><p>Veryl&#39;s own simulator holds a gate&#39;s enable for the whole edge, like a clock-gating cell, and does not report the rise after the commit. Celox does not follow it there; the shared suite records the difference for that simulator.</p><h2 id="cascaded-clocks" tabindex="-1">Cascaded clocks <a class="header-anchor" href="#cascaded-clocks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cascaded clocks&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A sequential update may itself create another clock edge:</p><div class="language-veryl vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">veryl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>always_ff (clk) {</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    clk_div = ~clk_div;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>always_ff (clk_div) {</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    cnt = cnt + 1;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>}</span></span></code></pre></div><p>After committing the first set of domains, Celox propagates combinational logic and examines triggered bits. Newly discovered domains are evaluated in another round of the same simulation step. The loop ends when no new event-producing change remains.</p><p>Conceptually, each round is:</p><ol><li>discover triggered domains;</li><li>evaluate every domain from the current Stable region;</li><li>commit their Working values together;</li><li>settle combinational logic and discover further triggers.</li></ol><p>The runtime may use a combined evaluate-and-commit kernel when exactly one domain can run without affecting cascade consistency. This is an implementation optimization and does not change the ordering model above.</p><h2 id="event-based-ticks" tabindex="-1">Event-based ticks <a class="header-anchor" href="#event-based-ticks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Event-based ticks&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>Simulator::tick</code> applies the same rules to one active edge of an event. Gated clocks of the event are judged from the state before the edge and evaluate with the ticked domain; after the commit, the rounds above run the domains whose clocks are computed from state and the gated clocks the commit moves. <code>Simulator::tick_events</code> does the same for coincident events, and the native testbench&#39;s <code>clock.next()</code> and the N-API <code>tick</code> use the same mechanism.</p><p>A tick is the event&#39;s active edge only, without simulation time, and does not change the event&#39;s signal. Domains that react to the signal&#39;s opposite edge, such as one clocked by <code>~clk</code> or by a falling edge of <code>clk &amp; en</code>, do not run on a tick; the timed <code>Simulation</code>, which drives both levels of a clock, runs them.</p><p><code>Simulator::posedge</code>, <code>negedge</code>, <code>active_edge</code>, <code>inactive_edge</code>, <code>cycle</code> and the coincident <code>edges</code> move the signal instead and apply the same rules to the transition they make, as a timed step does: the event&#39;s own domain runs on its active edge, gated clocks of either polarity are judged at the transition, and the signal stays at its new level. <code>cycle</code> is equivalent to the two timed steps of one clock period. The N-API handles and the WASM bridge of the TypeScript package use the same plans.</p><p>Which derived domains an event can reach is computed once when the design is built, together with a small program for each gate where its combinational cone allows: judging a gate then reads only the state it depends on instead of settling the design. Larger cones, and four-state designs, settle the design with the signal at each level instead. An event that reaches no derived domain takes the plain tick path with no added work.</p><h2 id="boundaries" tabindex="-1">Boundaries <a class="header-anchor" href="#boundaries" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Boundaries&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Combinational dependency cycles, including zero-delay cycles between clocks, are rejected during simulator construction.</li><li>Celox models RTL event behavior, not gate delays or a general-purpose SystemVerilog delta-cycle scheduler.</li></ul><p>User-facing guidance for dependency cycles is in <a href="/celox/guide/combinational-loops.html">Combinational Loops</a>.</p>`,28)])])}const g=a(o,[["render",i]]);export{m as __pageData,g as default};
